#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176719} :activity/id #uuid "6a297d5e-701c-5ce4-ab89-16c2883ce6f6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050816 :topic/id #uuid "68d7fe91-eb10-55fb-afa7-8dfde9672cfe" :topic/math-academy-id 3602} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176719 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176989 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $S$ is a surface parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\ndefined over a region $D$ in the $uv$-plane, then the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\: \\textrm{d}S =  \\iint\\limits_{D} \\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v  \\| \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v$ is the fundamental vector product of the surface.\n\nNow, if our surface $S$ is defined as $z= f(x,y),$ then it can always be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(x,y)= \\left\\langle x,\\: y,\\: f(x,y)\\right\\rangle  \\qquad (x,y)  \\in D.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the variables $x$ and $y$ act as parameters. \n\nThus, the fundamental vector product of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{'}\\times {r}_{y}^{'} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{∂f}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂f}{∂y},\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand using the fact that $z = f(x,y),$ we can write this as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{'}\\times {r}_{y}^{'} & =⟨-\\frac{∂z}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂z}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v  \\| = \\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that for a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nLet's look at an example of how to apply this formula." :tutorial/id #uuid "8319b2f4-f301-522e-944a-a56acf314d83" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5334 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "02eab857-ba70-5ffc-a6e2-2e77e9070917" :step/math-academy-id 33399 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176722}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175979 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175829 :question/id #uuid "bcdc7b9f-b5f8-5516-a0e6-8ca54fb80a48" :question/math-academy-id "e-8948"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278511}] :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"} :step/id #uuid "dafe3e1a-3347-5546-a43c-a510dbc307fa" :step/math-academy-id 33402 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176884}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176721 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175980 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175830 :question/id #uuid "80c2abda-a1d9-569e-936e-fc44ba77eb50" :question/math-academy-id "e-8959"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f4c9ee-6d8a-5a23-a28e-93f26081a32d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048384}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278402}] :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Rectangular Domains"} :step/id #uuid "40bd7598-b352-53c3-8ac4-1cc184b87de9" :step/math-academy-id 33440 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176722 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176990 :tutorial/content "![](images/86/867722106ffd8445b62a98ab43197ac2fba646faff95017ef7e92c254a97fb6e.png)\n\nThe surface $S$ shown above consists of the part of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ that lies under the plane $z=9.$ Let's compute the surface area of $S.$\n\nThe plane intersects the paraboloid when $z=9.$ Thus, the curve that describes the intersection points is\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = 9,\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $3$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nTherefore, our surface can be described by the equations\n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2, \\quad (x,y) \\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): \\, x^2 + y^2 \\le 9\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the surface area of $S,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions into our surface area formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by changing the variables to polar coordinates $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta),$ we have $\\textrm dA = r\\,\\textrm d r\\, \\textrm d \\theta.$ Thus, our integration region becomes\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta): \\, 0\\leq r\\leq 3,\\:0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+4(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta )}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}\\,}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,d\\theta \\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}\\,}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}\\,}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {[\\frac{1}{12}{(1+4{r}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{(1+4{r}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(37\\sqrt{37}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the area of our surface is \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}(37\\sqrt{37} -1).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1f3795a6-1971-55b3-9109-8565cd3830a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5344 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "20aafc3a-c734-59a5-9792-c59b83342b12" :step/math-academy-id 33441 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176721}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175996 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175848 :question/id #uuid "77e8fb3e-1858-5831-b740-a0c133c8180f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9141"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389167}] :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"} :step/id #uuid "dd0b86de-1ce8-5231-a10e-a2cb302f264c" :step/math-academy-id 34075 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176887}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176065 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175710 :question/id #uuid "7498df7d-a340-599e-b4ba-496f5142c28c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12379"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278409}] :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "95a582e7-cf2f-5cb4-a271-ae31cf32d50a" :step/math-academy-id 44417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177075 :tutorial/content "We have similar formulas for the areas of surfaces defined by $x = f(y,z)$ and $y = f(x,z){:}$\n\n- For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial y}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial z}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\n- For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial z}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c7f14be-f96a-5dc7-b3c9-10c1aa305b1c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7715 :tutorial/title "Cases Where the Domain Is the XZ- or YZ-Plane"} :step/id #uuid "a2e11edd-ffa0-5587-81ee-94d96ca815b9" :step/math-academy-id 44744 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176720}}] :activity/title "Areas of Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176142}]}
